                    "fan_type": "variable_speed",
                    "minimum_flow_fraction_howto": "HOW THE FAN TURNDOWN IS SET: not a field here. The measure floors the EnergyPlus Fan Power Minimum Flow Fraction at the lowest value in this scenario's stage_flow_fractions_heating / stage_flow_fractions_cooling (0.4), so the fan's claimed turndown always matches the staging this json specifies. The realised lowest stage can come out lower than that: adjust_cfm_per_ton_per_limits nudges lower stages to keep cfm/ton inside EnergyPlus's operating range (on a one-zone small office it moved variable-speed heating stage 1 from 0.40 to 0.28). That guard is a numerical safeguard, not a statement about how far the equipment turns down, so it is not allowed to lower fan power. EnergyPlus still moves the lower airflow; only the power curve is floored. A higher minimum outdoor-air ratio still wins over both.",
                    "fan_efficiency_howto": "HOW FAN EFFICIENCY IS BUILT: EnergyPlus fan total efficiency = impeller x motor. Only impeller_efficiency is set here, because it is a property of the equipment class this scenario represents. The motor is looked up at runtime by the measure from the fan's brake horsepower via openstudio-standards fan_standard_minimum_motor_efficiency_and_size (ASHRAE 90.1-2019 enclosed 4-pole table: 0.855 at <=1 hp, 0.865 at 1-2, 0.895 at 2-5, 0.917 at 5-10, 0.924 at 10-15, 0.930 at 15-20 hp), so total efficiency varies with fan size and is not a single number. Static pressure is carried over from the fan being replaced and is never set here. Minimum flow fraction is NOT stored here either: the measure derives it from the adjusted stage airflows so it tracks the cfm/ton limits.",
                    "fan_efficiency_range_for_this_scenario": "RESULTING TOTAL EFFICIENCY for this scenario: 0.65 x motor = 0.556 (<=1 hp) to 0.605 (15-20 hp), the same as two_speed because the impeller is held at 0.65 for every scenario (see impeller_efficiency_notes). The variable-speed fan advantage comes only from the part-load curve and the lower turndown floor, not from efficiency. Previously this scenario received 0.6045 from a no-op call to fan_change_motor_efficiency, i.e. LOWER than the standard-efficiency two-speed unit's 0.630, which was backwards.",
